WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.800
Hollywood has always managed to cash in with
genre pictures.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.040
When it comes to cinema's biggest bang for
the buck, however, studios big and small continue

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.020
to mine the spooky, blood-spattered realm
of horror.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.180
Few filmmakers have been able to get more
from a meager budget than Eduardo Sánchez

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:16.940
and Daniel Myrick.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.800
When the pair set out to turn their 35-page
outline into a feature film in 1997, they'd

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.180
cobbled together a mere $25,000.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.900
That modest budget gave the first-time filmmakers
just enough loot to hire three completely

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.800
unknown actors, drop them off in the woods
with a couple of borrowed cameras, and hope

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.350
that their efforts -- and an out-of-the-box
concept -- would be enough to turn that rough

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.720
outline into a genuinely unsettling found-footage
chiller.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.920
It was more than enough.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.239
The buzz behind The Blair Witch Project was
so real that audiences flocked to theaters

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.100
the summer of 1999 to see it.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.560
Many still believed it to be legit found footage,
which had a lot to do with a radical marketing

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.960
campaign that never quite confirmed or denied
the movie's authenticity.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.650
That the film itself is such a viscerally
unsettling experience in its own right only

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.780
fanned the flames, and the combination turned
Blair Witch into an unexpected box office

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.280
behemoth.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.980
With nearly $250 million in worldwide ticket
sales, it ranks among the most profitable

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:11.900
movies ever produced.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.900
John Carpenter's Halloween stands as one of
the most revered horror movies ever made.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.780
The near flawless 1978 film essentially gave
birth to the slasher genre, but it also paved

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.620
the way for meticulously crafted character-driven
scares.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.260
And as it happens, Carpenter's groundbreaking
slasher more than earns its spot on this list,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.460
because the Horror Master himself had a mere
$325,000 to bring his frightful vision to

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:34.960
life.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.520
That slim budget is, of course, part of what
helped Halloween stand out.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.851
It forced Carpenter to use innovative techniques,
like that steadicam perspective opening, or

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.540
taking a simple jumpsuit and a William Shatner
mask and turning it into a seemingly inhuman

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.040
monster.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.800
Though Carpenter and his many ghastly creations
have continued to spook us over the years,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.340
few have unsettled us quite like this micro-budget
masterpiece has.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.580
And even fewer have genuinely stood the test
of time with such monstrous eloquence.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.700
These days, we all know Jason Blum's Blumhouse
Productions as the powerhouse behind genre

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.079
hits like Sinister, Split, Get Out, and The
Purge.

00:02:09.079 --> 00:02:13.370
Prior to 2007, however, Blumhouse was known
mostly as a company that wanted to produce

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.020
high-quality genre movies with little to no
budget.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.460
With a few non-professional actors, a home
video camera, a seven-day shooting schedule,

00:02:20.469 --> 00:02:25.120
and a $15,000 budget, Paranormal Activity
didn't just fit the parameters for what Blumhouse

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.040
was trying to do, it essentially became the
blueprint.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.900
Oren Peli's haunting tale of a young couple
tormented by a malevolent demonic presence

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.600
was the film that officially put Blumhouse
on the Hollywood map.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.540
Once the film ran through Paramount Pictures
and into wide distribution, Paranormal Activity

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.640
scared up an astonishing $193 million at the
box office.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.459
"I don't think anyone anticipated it being
this successful"

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:49.029
For the record, those numbers also earned
the film the title of the most profitable

00:02:49.029 --> 00:02:49.660
movie ever made.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.940
With eyebrow-raising numbers like that, it
wasn't long before people started throwing

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:53.920
the word "sequel" around.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.520
Instead, Peli and Blumhouse's stark, no-budget
vision birthed an entire franchise.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.339
To date, the Paranormal Activity-verse has
spawned a total of five sequels that have

00:03:02.339 --> 00:03:05.400
brought in close to a billion dollars in worldwide
ticket sales.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.820
Not a bad return on an initial $15,000 investment.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.620
Though he's dabbled almost exclusively in
the realm of tentpole pictures over the last

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.380
couple of decades, Sam Raimi spent the early
days of his career as a low-budget genre wiz.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.180
With a dizzying penchant for delivering both
big laughs and bloody carnage with equal fervor,

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:28.159
1981's ghoul-fest The Evil Dead delivered
a genuinely disturbing, altogether unforgettable

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:29.510
cinematic experience.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:34.540
Raimi put together the effects-heavy film
with a reported budget of only $350,000.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:38.980
While it didn't exactly set the domestic box
office ablaze, The Evil Dead more than turned

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.010
a tidy profit upon release, and helped set
a new standard for the growing wave of shock

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.920
and schlock horror devotees.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.659
More than anything, it proved Raimi to be
a filmmaker of singular cinematic vision,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.760
and it remains ground zero for the wild stylistic
flourishes, unapologetic camp, and relentless

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.790
bloodletting that would inform virtually every
one of his films thereafter.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.260
Oh, and did we mention that The Evil Dead
also made a bona fide cult sensation of its

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.980
chisel-chinned star Bruce Campbell?

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:08.220
Make no mistake, when George A. Romero's zombie
classic Night of the Living Dead hit theaters

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.540
back in 1968, it was like nothing that had
ever come before it.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.810
The film was so singular in vision and execution
that it basically invented a new horror subgenre,

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.470
and moviegoers have been devouring tales of
the flesh-eating undead ever since.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.000
"They're coming to get you, Barbara"

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.280
Of course, Night of the Living Dead wasn't
always meant to be "game-changing horror."

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.160
Romero's feature debut was originally supposed
to be a sci-fi comedy about hot-rodding aliens.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.940
When budget constraints made that concept
all but impossible, the idea pivoted to flesh-eating

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:37.440
aliens.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.080
It finally ended up on undead, flesh-eating
humans, which allowed the production to make

00:04:41.081 --> 00:04:44.370
full use of its paltry $114,000 budget.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.080
To say Romero got his money's worth would
be an understatement.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.860
The film's gritty style, grisly special effects,
and biting social commentary hit home with

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:57.169
genre fans and casual filmgoers alike, and
brought in a staggering $18 million at the

00:04:57.169 --> 00:04:58.100
box office.

